Subject: Description of the icons/shortcuts in the Commands Window
To: pepswin <Unknown>


Everything starts in the Commands window.  [Note: I keep only a desktop 
shortcut to the Commands window on the desktop so there is virtually no 
clutter.]

This will be in top to bottom, left to right order.

The top row is operating system oriented.  More can appear in future 
releases if considered important enough.  If not, they are relegated to the 
first one called Other Systems.  Users have to choose what is important to 
them, a balance of functionality and lack of clutter.  For the moment, all 
you will find in there is references to the pure paper-tape operating mode 
of SIMH and two icons for a very old version of P?S/8 that is about 14 
years of development work older than the current release.  For those 
familiar with the R-L Monitor, this represents more of a "transition" 
between the progenitor system from 1967 [toggled into memory by Richard 
Lary and Lenny Elekman, with help and encouragement from Hank Maurer, Joe 
Fischetti and Jack Burness].  Future releases will likely add on the actual 
R-L Monitor to see the full span of development history.  Once you use the 
newest version of P?S/8 [Version 8Z] and then this older one [8T[ you will 
be prepped enough to want to try the original [as all of us "grew up" on, 
etc.  [Note: Version 8T does have a pretty good manual, nearly as good as 
the one for Version 8Z; however, there is only some scant pages some of us 
wrote back in the day for when R-L was submitted to DECUS as MS/8, so you 
really have to be comfortable with 8T as a prerequisite for that potential 
journey back in time to the dawn of the personal computer era [which 
arguably it is].  I could also add POLY BASIC, which is a stand-alone BASIC 
system that was entirely DEVELOPED on the R-L Monitor [!].  But for now the 
point is either stick your icons on this row of the desktop OR "inside" of 
the Other Systems icon which opens that window separately.

Note:  There is a personal preference setting in all Windows about open all 
Windows in their own window on the desktop OR all open in the SAME 
desktop-based window.  It is highly recommended to use the separate window 
approach, at least until you get used to how this works.  [And of course, 
if you want to use details view on everything, have at it and ignore all 
the suggestions!  I just know that I am a fan of details view and I am NOT 
choosing that option...]

OS/8.  This starts the general configuration of both P?S/8 and OS/8 at the 
same time in OS/8.  OS/8 boots on the RKA0: and RKB0: drive, and generally 
the OS/8-specific .ini file specifies some useful additional drives for 
RKA1:, RKB1:, RKA2:, RKB2:, and RKA3:, RKB3: as needed.  You have to edit 
your own copy if you want different drives mounted, etc.  [Note:  It is 
recommended that if you choose to modify the file, definitely create a 
clean backup of the original.  Changing SIMH .ini files is not for those 
faint of heart!]

In this configuration, P?S/8 is configured to be bootable on TC08 drive 0.  
[Note: P?S/8 Version 8Z can actually be booted to ANY drive 0 through 
7;there is an extended bootstrap available; however, OS/8 can only use it 
for strictly drive 0 purposes. I can post the short source code for a 
viable version that uses the front panel switches to select which drive you 
want to boot from, etc.]

There are a total of eight P?S/8-relevant DECtape drives.  For the present 
release [due to some implementation restrictions that will go away in the 
next release] unit 7 must be a scratch drive.  Arbitrarily, unit one is 
reserved as the "go-between" drive to allow the P?S/8 OS8CON utility to 
bidirectionally transfer PAL and related source files between the two 
systems.]  To access this P?S/8 system, from OS/8 use BOOT/DT.

PQS/8

[Note: Windows does not allow a ? in the icon captioning text.  Actually, 
it also doesn't allow a "/" either!  the character you see is not a "real" 
slash character, but rather an oddball that is allowed that is pretty 
close!]

Since we cannot use a "?" on icons, we resort to the standard naming of 
P?S/8:  If you cannot express a ?, use a Q, but always express it verbally 
as Pee-Queue-Ess slash ate,  In file names, you also have to drop the slash 
character, so this is pervasive throughout PEPS and these considerations 
also apply to OS/8, etc.

This is the same configuration as the OS/8 above, except it boots to P?S/8 
DECtape 0 to start the latest P?S/8 system [which is Version 8Z for 
TC01/TC08 and 4K minimum.   In many ways, this is the "flagship" P/S/8 
configuration which was demoed to DEC years ago and showed just how slow 
OS/8 is.  [OS/8 PAL8 ran on RK8E/RK05.  P?S/8 on just a few DECtapes.  PAL8 
was twice as fast as P?S/8 running on drives that transfer far, far 
slower!  More profoundly P?S/8 BIN using the Slurp loader, loaded binary 
FASTER than OS/8 ABSLDR loading the identical binary!]  [Note: The SIMH 
does NOT simulate in real-time; there is a setting to partially do that, 
and it is set, but it doesn't have the "feel" of the real thing; too fast!]

PQS/8 (LPT Disabled)

P?S/8 handles the system line printer differently from OS/8,m and instead 
is the same conceptually as DIAL-MS for the PDP-12 [and incidentally and 
ironically OS/8 BATCH which is NOT compatible with the rest of OS/8!].

In OS/8 [other than OS/8 BATCH] attempts to use the LPT are explicitly done 
by referencing the LPT: handler for the device 66 printer hardware. [And 
admittedly you can configure for a few alternate choices.]  Thus, what you 
ask for is what you get - always.

In P?S/8, programs that can utiliize the LPT: do so by discovery of the 
hardware and time-out.  As such, you have to disable the hardware to cause 
output on the console.

In the days of P?S/8 yore [such as Version 8T] few programs had an override 
switch to force which way the output went.  As of Version 8Z, most or at 
least many actually support a command-line switch to inhibit the 
LPT:output.  [Jote: Future releases may invert the switch to instead mean 
allow LPT checking and defaults to the system console terminal if the LPT: 
is not available, but this is a minor and tentative detail.  Version 9A has 
a whole lot of other things to be done before considering a minor point 
such as this, etc.]

As such, this setting is far more important in an older system such as 
Version 8T from the 1970.s  [P?S/8 development predates the existence of 
OS/2 being started! In 1969, the R-L Monitor was already nearly three years 
old, and it was time to replace it.  OS/8 wasn't even yet a bad dream at 
that point!].

In any case, since there may be some stragglers, this variant is provided 
and of course it means it is moot to even provide the /T switch even if 
implemented; it will output to the console anyway, etc.

Before considering all on the second line there is a need to explain the 
third line:

the third line is primarily customized icons for some specific projects. 
This is but one of the conceptual ways to approach program development in 
PEPS which encourages Windows host editing and file storage; for differing 
reasons, both OS/8 and P?S/8 [as presently implemented, but this will 
change in the future, OS/8 will never change] are quite unsuitable, 
especially if interchanging files from other sites [or e-mail, etc.].  This 
will make sense below when I get to the sixth icon explanation, etc.

Note: I am considering swapping the two pairs of icons on rows two and 
three.  Please comment on this as I am torn between which pair is more 
appropriate.  If they are swapped, perhaps there is some more "sense", but 
to do so makes row two have more disparate concepts all in the same place.  
[This was recently pointed out to me, and the screen shot, while new, 
predates this person's input, so don't be surprised if a larger consensus 
convinces be to swap the last two from 2 and three with each other, etc.]

Now to Row 2.

Edit Current Program.  The easiest way to approach the PEPS package IF you 
wish to work with existing source files [or write your own or edit other 
existing files, etc.] is to use the generic notion of THE Current Program 
[using a name you designate].  The only restriction is that it must reside 
in the Current_Program_Files directory inside of the central directory tree 
of PEPS itself.]  It is very easy to setup which [single] file to use.  
Thus, this opens your chosen file for editing in preparation for sending it 
[later] to the SIMH customly configured.according to your preference.  
[Note: There are underlying .BAT files that control this.  The default in 
the release is that will be OS/8 passed this file in the PTR: input 
stream.  This can be changed to ANY of the available choices, such as P?S/8 
Version 8Z with the LPT: enabled.]

EVERY PEPS program project has two icons.  Here is why:

The Edit icon sets up the PTR: input stream with its corresponding file, 
but only when you CLOSE the editor.  This is actually optional.

Send Current Program

This sets up the PTR: input using the PRESENT value of the designated 
program and then runs the designated O/S configuration, in this case by 
default OS/8 [but can be edited to do any of them].

Thus, the second way to use the Edit partner icon is to just SAVE the file 
and NOT close the editor.  Then, leaving the edit window open, use the SEND 
icon to get SIMH up and the current file contents are now in the PTR: 
stream [actually it's a file in SIMH of all frames you can grab with a PTR: 
handler or equivalent, etc.

This is the preferred way for "rapid-fire" development.  When you are at an 
appropriate point, SAVE the file, but keep the editor open.  Then SEND it 
to SIMH, process accordingly, likely leading to some output to either the 
LPT: or PTP: in some instances, etc.  Work with that and then revisit the 
editing process, repeating everything as necessary. Eventually, just close 
the editor when you are no longer performing any more updates [for now].

This technique is actually faster than doing similar on the real hardware!

But you can instead close the editor and then just run OS/8 as described 
above.

There is an additional advantage however:  You could INSTEAD just run P?S/8 
and work on the file there. Personally, I use both, and for me, the tide is 
turning towards P?S/8 as the number of reasons I used OS/8 at all has 
dwindled.  Other than the editing and storing of files, there is little 
OS/8 offers me because so many things are either equivalent in both systems 
or P?S/8 has definite advantages.  In any case, this way does NOT involve 
changing any ini files or anything else as "drastic" but it does mean you 
have to NOT use the SEND icon [which can only go to the designated system; 
this gets you multiple choices, etc.

Set Current Program.  This is the utility where you setup the actual file 
name of what you want to be the Current Program. A screen appears with an 
auxiliary Windows-type dialogue box to allow entering the name of a file.  
This must be done carefully, as the only restriction is do not use embedded 
spaces in the name, etc.  It is perfectly OK to specify a new file here; it 
will be created in the Current_Program_Files directory within the PEPS 
folders directory tree, etc.

List Current Programs.

This creates an output text files of a a DOS prompt DIR command.  Because 
it is a text file, you can copy and paste at will, such as to use in the 
aforementioned dialogue box as either whole or in part what you want to set 
the latest current file name to, etc. [The dialogue box is an a 
command-line buffer, copy and paste and then hand tweak it as you see fit.]

Explore Current Programs.

This opens Windows Explorer in the Current+Program_Files directory.  Using 
Windows explorer copy [or perhaps cut] and Paste techniques, you can get a 
file from somewhere else into the Current+Program_Files directory..

More Custom Options.

As stated earlier, this icon is subject to being moved to perhaps the third 
line.  It opens a subordinate window of other icon pairings that are 
contributed by other users of PEPS for their projects.  [Note: While all of 
these pairs of icons function, they may point to dummy files provided to 
avoid errors.  It would be inappropriate to house these files in the 
package, and there may be proprietary reasons to avoid direct original 
intended functionality.  That said, the more examples of how to make custom 
icon.

Explore Additional Files.

This allows navigation to other directories to allow windows techniques to 
be used to move around files at will.  the default directory is the 
Source_Files directory within the PEPS folders directory tree.  Some of 
these directories are functional and might even unclude several "live" 
files as used by others [and me personally].  [Note: My personal copy is 
quite a lot larger containing many PDP-8-oriented files from many sources, 
including active word-processing projects of PDP-8 documentation files, 
etc.].

Clearly this is useful to be used in conjuction with the Explore Current 
Directory icon as described above, etc.

I will close this file and start another one.

cjl [baby steps]


